{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791811921,"nonce":"1u9U/RydxLzCdZhgGFolnMAmCJs=","title":"Braced Unicode and hexadecimal string escapes are not decoded","message":"The shared parser unescape_string always reads four characters after \\u and does not handle \\x. UnicodeSequences.validString contains \\u{10000} through \\u{10FFFF} and emoji; Rayzor renders tails such as \"00}\" instead of the UTF-8 scalars. Both the recursive-descent and nom parsers use this decoder.\n\nThe isolated UnicodeEscapes.hx checks UTF-8 bytes for \\u{1F602} and \\u{10FFFF}, plus \\x70. Native Haxe 4.3.6 passes; the frozen v60 Rayzor binary throws \"braced Unicode\". With actual filesystem test data generated, TestUnicode's I/O probe reproduces the corrupted literal against the correct file bytes.\n\nEvidence: target/conformance-checkpoint/unicode-escape-probe/UnicodeEscapes.hx, unicode-escape-v60-baseline.log and unicode-io-generated-v60e.log. Preserve existing fixed-width \\uXXXX escapes and ordinary string/interpolation behavior.","files":null}]}